we begin with the surging omicron  variant which is spreading at a rapid  rate in the united kingdom health  experts warn that it's the most  significant threat to public health  since the start of this pandemic and on  wednesday the uk recorded its highest  ever daily number of cases more than 78  thousand dw correspondent charlotte  chelsea pill reports from london  [Music]  festive cheer on the streets of london  once again ringing hollow  shops and hospitality may all be open  christmas parties still allowed to go  ahead as planned  but covert is casting its dark shadow  over the days ahead as experts warn the  omicron tidal wave is coming and it's  coming at a phenomenal pace  what we know  is that it reproduces very very fast um  a week ago or so we said it  reproduces every two to three days or  three times a week and putting it very  simply that means in one week you get  eight times as many infections two weeks  sixty four times as many three weeks 512  times as many so it's coming at us like  an express train  the uk is already recording  record-covered cases with omicron now  the dominant variant here in london  resisting calls from some for further  restrictions the government's focusing  instead on its booster campaign  resulting in lengthy cues at vaccine  sites across the country  obviously the omicron is growing so  rapidly in london and we just wanted to  get in as quickly as possible so many  people that i've heard of are getting  covert again  from last week i'd have there was an  office party of 14 16 of them got  covered clearly omicron is really  spreading quickly it's very relevant or  very important for me to come here today  for all five hours  not that i want to make an issue of it  but um i live with my mother and father  who have been there ages i wanted to  ideally get it done before i went to go  and see  relatives and people over christmas i  can't feel my toes though but it's okay  it's okay  the government's throwing the doors open  to every adult in england to get boosted  by the end of the year they're hoping to  offer up to a million jabs a day as  early data suggests the third vaccine is  key to combating omagra this is a  massive undertaking for the health  service they're being asked for another  major vaccine push to try and prevent  hospitals like this one from being  overwhelmed but there are concerns it  won't be enough as people here line up  for their boosters there are already  people in hospital with omicron  hospitalizations are climbing and one  person's confirmed to have died with  omicron  while it's not yet clear how severe the  variant is the sheer number of cases  alone could prove overwhelming  in the shadow of this vaccine center a  reminder of what's at stake  a memorial wall commemorating the tens  of thousands of lives lost to covid  and it's with the pain and sacrifice of  the past that this weary nation is now  facing the threat of another worrying  winter to come  our next guest is danny altman he's a  professor of immunology in london and he  joins us now mr altman thank you for  taking the time to speak with us i want  to start with the report that we just  saw where charlotte outlined the very  severe situation that the united kingdom  finds itself right now due to the  omicron variant which is now the  dominant variant in london based on  everything you've seen so far do you  think that this british government has  done enough  i think  scientists and experts were pleased to  see them doing something and going to um  to their plan b so in other words more  behavioral measures  and um greater emphasis on boosting um  whether we've done enough any time will  tell this looks really really serious  and we probably need to do more  do you think that the fact that they  lifted the mask mandate some time ago  which then now they've reinstated do you  think that that hurt efforts  i think it did um  i think um you know we we have this um  discussion don't we about whether this  is all about vaccine boosters or whether  it's about vaccine boosters plus all the  behavioral measures like masks and the  answer is it's absolutely about both um  in this country we kind of had our  freedom day and and took our foot off  the gas and now we need to revisit some  of those choices  the freedom day that you're referring to  was announced by prime minister boris  johnson back in the summer where all the  restrictions were lifted and he said  we're going to return to normal and  clearly that hasn't been the case and it  certainly isn't the case now leading up  into christmas what is your biggest  concern mr altman now in the united  kingdom as we approach christmas and  potentially more travel  well you know your items has covered the  terms hasn't it like um tidal wave and  express train  and that's what's coming to us it's  coming to much of the rest of the world  and something that's so infectious and  spreading through the population so fast  can really devastate us both in terms of  its death toll and its effect on health  services  talk to us a little bit more about the  impact on health services doctors nurses  have gone through much already in the  nearly past two years and now they're  facing this omicron variant and some  people are not willing to get vaccinated  what kind of impact could this have now  in the coming weeks and months with  regard to the health system in the  united kingdom  it's so extreme you know we're now two  years into this aren't we of a very  exhausted and beleaguered and injured  and overstretched health service um and  i think people here a um logistically  aren't in a position to cope anymore and  b simply don't have the capacity in  terms of hospital beds and intensive  care beds to deal with the kind of surge  that it looks like we're facing  dr danny altman professor of immunology  in the imperial college of london thank  you very much for your time  so that is the situation in britain but  what about the rest of the world let's  bring in trolls lilibeck he is the head  of denmark's coronavirus variant  assessment committee a very warm welcome  to the show professor denmark has one of  europe's best virus screening programs  in terms of sequencing and in that sense  it can provide something of an early  warning system for what's ahead so tell  us what is coming for the rest of us  with omicron  what we also see in denmark is is that  the rates are doubling every second or  third day so we are seeing you know a  lot of testing positive every day we  have new record last  24 hours nearly 9 000 cases and we have  also  a larger and larger proportion of those  cases are omicron cases  so breaking records we know omicron  spreads like wildfire but we're still  not sure if it's actually more harmful  than let's say the delta variant is that  correct  yeah yeah that's correct that we're not  sure but i think even if it's half as  harmful as the delta variant the problem  is the total number of cases because if  we reach a very very high number of  cases that would be tenth of thousands  in denmark then even if it's half as  dangerous still the total strain on the  hospital system will be extremely high  and a lot of the staff members in  hospital nursing rooms all those kind of  functions they have to go home because  they test positive perhaps they have  mild symptoms but still they cannot take  care of the other ones in the hospital  or those who need help so so that's the  problem anyway  so just be clear the biggest threat that  we know that it could be coming with  omicron is that you'll have too many  people infected at the same time uh even  if it's not as serious some of those  will be showing up in hospitals and all  overwhelming the hospital system at the  same time  exactly yes that that is the problem so  so we we do not know if it is it's  causing less disease than delta but but  you could say it doesn't really matter  because if you get all the cases at the  same time and then you have a very  severe problem so that is what we are  trying to avoid right now in denmark by  very very huge vaccination campaigns we  have different meshes in place  and and and do all kinds of things in  order to not have all the cases at the  same time to get distributed on on more  days or weeks so to say  let's talk about those campaigns because  we keep hearing that vaccination is key  also now that two jabs aren't enough  against omicron virologists are urging  everyone to get a third booster shot but  is that really the best strategy given  that in many countries especially in the  global south the majority of people  haven't even had their first job  no i think what  we know now for for certain that when  you get your boosted errors then you  have a good effect about  symptomatic infections and you have a  good effect about uh hospitalization and  and what we all feel that would be to to  get hospitalized to get severely sick so  so i think that that is the best weapon  also because it seems that omicron  spreads very easily so so so it will be  difficult to avoid the the this variant  just just by restrictions you still need  the restrictions not to get this very  very high peak at once or at least  trying to avoid it but but but i would  say that vaccinations is the best  defense even after two vaccines it seems  that there is some effect against  serious disease thanks to these memory  cells so it's not all about antibodies  but but vaccinations are certainly the  key  now one of the kinds of restrictions  that governments are imposing is to try  and limit movement and we learned today  that france is restricting travel uh to  from the uk to try and curb the spread  of omicron how much good is that going  to do can we not already assume that the  variant is all over europe and could  become the dominant strain here soon  absolutely i think  the variant is already there i think  it's a matter of how much you look after  it in the society in denmark we do 200  000 pt pcr tests per day an additional  250 000 rapid tests and remember we have  a population of 5.8 million so that's  nearly half million tests per day so we  discover omicron at a very very early  stage and in the beginning it's doubling  from you know two to four four to eight  eight to 16 and so on when you have a  delta outbreak at the same time you will  not discover the initial doubling so i  think that's happening in in many  european countries someday unless they  monitor very closely what is happening  on all the positive cases so so at some  point suddenly you know omicron will  peak through the the delta cases so i'm  sure that that is happening most places  in europe right now you see the norway  you say in uk you see it in denmark  where we do a lot of genotyping and and  do a lot of pcr  the whole population tested nearly every  week or something like that  so so so it's already there i don't  think travel restrictions is the way to  go now it just doesn't really make any  sense charles lillaback the head of  denmark's chronovirus variant assessment  committee thanks so much for coming on  the show with those insights  you're welcome  now for a breakdown of some pandemic  developments elsewhere in the world  south korea has authorized its toughest  pandemic restrictions to date starting  saturday private gatherings will be  limited to five people schools will go  back to remote learning and restaurants  will close by 9 p.m and here in germany  there are plans to buy 92 million  vaccine doses for the new year the  announcement comes after the new health  minister warned of a jab shortage in the  first quarter of 2022. new zealand says  90 of its eligible population is now  fully inoculated the nation's health  regulator has also granted provisional  approval for the pfizer biontech vaccine  for five to 11 year olds  you

